## Why This Is Asked
This question tests knowledge of runtime extensibility, per-endpoint configuration, and safe concurrency control in distributed systems. The candidate must justify pattern choices, discuss trade-offs in dynamic environments, and demonstrate awareness of deadlock prevention and backpressure strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for encapsulating concurrency behavior
- Registry + Factory pattern for runtime-extensible policy injection
- Per-endpoint isolation with sensible defaults
- Deadlock avoidance through careful resource acquisition ordering and bounded queues
- Backpressure considerations to prevent memory exhaustion under load

## Code Example
```typescript
interface ConcurrencyPolicy {
  submit(job: () => void | Promise<void>): void;
  start(): void;
  stop(): void;
}

interface PolicyFactory {
  create(config?: Record<string, unknown>): ConcurrencyPolicy;
}

class PolicyRegistry {
  private factories = new Map<string, PolicyFactory>();

  register(name: string, factory: PolicyFactory): void {
    this.factories.set(name, factory);
  }

  create(name: string, config?: Record<string, unknown>): ConcurrencyPolicy {
    const factory = this.factories.get(name);
    if (!factory) throw new Error(`Unknown policy: ${name}`);
    return factory.create(config);
  }
}

// Built-in policies
class FixedWorkerPool implements ConcurrencyPolicy {
  private queue: Array<() => void> = [];
  private active = 0;

  constructor(private maxWorkers: number) {}

  submit(job: () => void): void {
    if (this.active < this.maxWorkers) {
      this.active++;
      job();
    } else {
      this.queue.push(job);
    }
  }

  start(): void { /* drain queue */ }
  stop(): void { /* reject queued */ }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PolicyRegistry();
registry.register('fixed', (config) => new FixedWorkerPool(config?.workers ?? 10));

const runner = new TaskRunner(registry);
runner.assign('POST /orders', 'fixed', { workers: 20 });
```

## Trade-off Discussion
| Aspect | FixedWorkerPool | WorkStealingPool | BandwidthLimitedPool |
|--------|-----------------|------------------|----------------------|
| Throughput | Predictable, bounded | High under skew | Rate-limited |
| Latency | Moderate queue delay | Low for varied work | Controlled but variable |
| Complexity | Low | High (deques, synchronization) | Medium (token bucket) |
| Deadlock Risk | Low if bounded queue | Medium (circular stealing) | Low |
| Memory | Bounded by queue size | Unbounded steals possible | Bounded by token rate |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests runtime extensibility and correctness of flag evaluation, including versioning and thread-safety.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for evaluation logic
- Registry/Factory for runtime additions
- Thread-safety and version-keying
- Performance implications of dynamic dispatch

## Code Example
```typescript
interface User { id: string; segments: string[]; }
interface Flag { name: string; version: string; defaultValue?: boolean; }

interface FlagEvaluationStrategy {
  evaluate(user: User, flag: Flag): boolean;
}
type StrategyFactory = () => FlagEvaluationStrategy;

class FlagRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, StrategyFactory>();
  register(key: string, factory: StrategyFactory) { this.map.set(key, factory); }
  get(key: string): FlagEvaluationStrategy {
    const f = this.map.get(key);
    if (!f) throw new Error(`No strategy registered for ${key}`);
    return f();
  }
}
class FlagEvaluator {
  constructor(private registry: FlagRegistry) {}
  eval(flag: Flag, user: User): boolean {
    const strat = this.registry.get(`${flag.name}|${flag.version}`);
    return strat.evaluate(user, flag);
  }
}
class PercentStrategy implements FlagEvaluationStrategy {
  constructor(private percent: number) {}
  evaluate(user: User, flag: Flag): boolean {
    const r = (parseInt(user.id.slice(-4), 10) % 100) < this.percent;
    return r;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Explores runtime extensibility using the Decorator and Registry patterns in a practical CLI context.

## Key Concepts
- **Registry pattern**: Centralized storage for cross-cutting concerns
- **Decorator pattern**: Wraps commands in layers for logging, retry, and metrics
- **Per-command isolation**: Faults in one decorator don't cascade to others
- **Runtime composition**: Decorator chains are assembled at execution time, not compile time

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Command {
  execute(ctx: any): Promise<void>;
}

interface Decorator extends Command {
  setNext(cmd: Command): Command;
}

class DecoratorRegistry {
  private decorators: Decorator[] = [];

  register(decorator: Decorator) {
    this.decorators.push(decorator);
  }

  compose(cmd: Command): Command {
    return this.decorators.reduceRight(
      (inner, decorator) => decorator.setNext(inner),
      cmd
    );
  }
}

const registry = new DecoratorRegistry();

registry.register({
  setNext(cmd) {
    return {
      execute: async (ctx) => {
        console.log('Logging:', ctx);
        await cmd.execute(ctx);
      },
    };
  },
} as Decorator);

class CommandFactory {
  private commands = new Map<string, Command>();

  register(name: string, cmd: Command) {
    this.commands.set(name, registry.compose(cmd));
  }

  execute(name: string, ctx: any) {
    return this.commands.get(name)!.execute(ctx);
  }
}

const factory = new CommandFactory();
factory.register('deploy', {
  execute: async (ctx) => console.log('Deploying:', ctx),
});

factory.execute('deploy', { env: 'prod' });
```

## Key Takeaway
Adding a new cross-cutting concern requires only registering a new decorator—no modifications to existing commands or the factory.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a runtime-extendable pipeline, ensuring immutability, async handling, and per-transform error reporting. It also exercises runtime registration and ordering without redeploys.

## Key Concepts

- Registry pattern and dynamic lookup
- Pipeline composition with explicit order
- Immutability and async error handling
- Observability of per-transform failures

## Code Example

```javascript
interface Transformer<T> {
  name: string
  apply(input: T): Promise<T>
}
class TransformerRegistry<T> {
  private map = new Map<string, Transformer<T>>()
  register(t: Transformer<T>): void { this.map.set(t.name, t) }
  get(name: string): Transformer<T> | undefined { return this.map.get(name) }
}
class Pipeline<T> {
  constructor(private reg: TransformerRegistry<T>, private order: string[]) {}
  async run(input: T): Promise<{out: T; errors: string[]}> {
    let acc = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(input)) as T
    const errors: string[] = []
    for (const name of this.order) {
      const t = this.reg.get(name)
      if (!t) continue
      try { acc = await t.apply(acc) }
      catch (e) { errors.push(name + ': ' + (e?.message ?? 'err')) }
    }
    return { out: acc, errors }
  }
}
```

